<source id="1" url="https://chat.codercops.com/server/express/" title="Express Adapter" section="Basic Setup &gt; Behind a proxy">
Express Adapter > Basic Setup > Behind a proxy

Rate limits and the per-IP budget use `req.ip`. Behind a reverse proxy or a load balancer, `req.ip` is the proxy's address unless you tell Express to trust it, and then every visitor shares one limit. With one proxy in front:

```typescript
import express from 'express';

const app = express();
app.set('trust proxy', 1);
```

The server logs a warning when requests carry `X-Forwarded-For` but `trust proxy` is off. Don't set it to `true` unless every request comes through your proxy: Express would then believe any `X-Forwarded-For` a visitor sends.
</source>
<source id="2" url="https://chat.codercops.com/server/security/" title="Security" section="A production config &gt; Express">
Security > A production config > Express

```typescript
import express from 'express';
import pg from 'pg';
import { chatcopsMiddleware } from '@chatcops/server';
import { postgresStorage } from '@chatcops/store-postgres';

const app = express();
// Behind one proxy (a load balancer, nginx), so req.ip is the visitor's.
app.set('trust proxy', 1);
app.use(express.json());

app.use(
  '/api/chat',
  chatcopsMiddleware({
    provider: { type: 'claude', apiKey: process.env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY! },
    systemPrompt: 'You are the Acme support assistant.',
    storage: postgresStorage({ db: new pg.Pool({ connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL }) }),
    allowedOrigins: ['https://acme.com', 'https://www.acme.com'],
    sessionSecrets: [process.env.CHATCOPS_SESSION_SECRET!],
    budgets: { globalPerDay: { tokens: 20_000_000 } },
  })
);
```

