<source id="4" url="https://chat.codercops.com/getting-started/upgrading/" title="Upgrading from 0.5" section="Things to check &gt; Per-IP limits need the visitor's IP">
Upgrading from 0.5 > Things to check > Per-IP limits need the visitor's IP

Rate limits and the per-IP budget key on the visitor's IP. The server now reads it only from headers the platform sets itself. On Vercel and Cloudflare that just works. Express uses `req.ip`, which follows `app.set('trust proxy', ...)`. Anywhere else (Netlify, a Node server behind your own proxy, Bun, Deno), set `clientIp` to read your host's header. Until you do, per-IP limits are skipped, with one warning. The server used to trust the first `X-Forwarded-For` entry, which a visitor can set to anything. See [the visitor's IP](/server/fetch-runtimes/#the-visitors-ip).
</source>
<source id="5" url="https://chat.codercops.com/server/fetch-runtimes/" title="Next.js, Hono, Bun and Deno" section="The visitor's IP">
Next.js, Hono, Bun and Deno > The visitor's IP

Rate limits and the per-IP budget key on the visitor's IP. The handler only reads it from a header that the platform sets itself, because a visitor can send any `X-Forwarded-For` they like and get a fresh limit with every request.

| Where it runs | Where the IP comes from |
|---|---|
| Vercel | Vercel's own headers (`x-vercel-forwarded-for`, `x-real-ip`, `x-forwarded-for`), which Vercel overwrites. Nothing to set. |
| Cloudflare Workers (`chatcopsCloudflareHandler`) | `CF-Connecting-IP`. Nothing to set. |
| Express | `req.ip`, which follows Express's `trust proxy` setting. Behind one proxy, call `app.set('trust proxy', 1)`. |
| Anywhere else | Your `clientIp` function. |

Anywhere else, set `clientIp` to read the header your host or proxy sets:

```typescript
import { chatcopsVercelHandler } from '@chatcops/server';

const handler = chatcopsVercelHandler({
  provider: { type: 'claude', apiKey: process.env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY! },
